
# Java Reactor Homework — Answers

## 1. Why does the thread-per-request model waste resources for I/O-bound work?

In the traditional thread-per-request model, each incoming HTTP request is assigned a dedicated thread. The thread handles the entire request, including database queries and external API calls.

For example, consider the following timeline:

```text
Time       Thread                  Operation
--------------------------------------------------
0 ms       Thread-1                Receive HTTP request
10 ms      Thread-1                Send database query
10-210 ms  Thread-1 (BLOCKED)      Wait for database response
210 ms     Thread-1                Process database result
220 ms     Thread-1                Call external API
220-720 ms Thread-1 (BLOCKED)      Wait for API response
720 ms     Thread-1                Build HTTP response
```

During the database and external API calls, the thread spends most of its time waiting for I/O rather than performing useful CPU work.

This wastes resources because:

1. Each thread consumes memory for its stack and other runtime structures.
2. A large number of concurrent requests requires many threads.
3. Thread scheduling and context switching introduce overhead.
4. Thread pools can become exhausted when many requests are waiting for I/O.

For example, if 1,000 requests are simultaneously waiting for database responses, a traditional thread-per-request server may need up to 1,000 request-handling threads to support them concurrently.

A reactive system uses non-blocking I/O. Instead of keeping a thread blocked while waiting, it registers an operation and allows the thread to process other events.

When the database or external API responds, the system resumes processing through an event-driven callback.

**Conclusion:** The thread-per-request model wastes resources for I/O-bound applications because threads remain occupied while waiting. Non-blocking I/O improves resource efficiency by allowing a smaller number of threads to handle many concurrent requests.

