## 6. Why does try/catch around pipeline construction not catch reactive errors?

Reactive pipelines are usually executed later, when a subscriber subscribes.

A try/catch around pipeline construction only catches exceptions thrown synchronously while constructing the pipeline.

It does not catch errors emitted asynchronously during execution.

For example:

```java
try {
    Mono<Integer> mono =
        Mono.fromCallable(() -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("Database error");
        });
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Caught error");
}
```

The exception is not thrown during pipeline construction.

It occurs when the Mono is subscribed to.

Reactor provides operators for handling these errors.

### onErrorReturn

Returns a fixed fallback value when an error occurs.

```java
Mono.just(10)
    .map(n -> n / 0)
    .onErrorReturn(-1);
```

If an error occurs, the Mono emits `-1`.

### onErrorResume

Switches to another Publisher when an error occurs.

```java
getProduct()
    .onErrorResume(error -> getFallbackProduct());
```

This is useful when the fallback requires additional logic or another asynchronous operation.

### onErrorMap

Transforms an error into another exception.

```java
getProduct()
    .onErrorMap(error ->
        new RuntimeException("Product lookup failed", error));
```

This is useful for translating low-level exceptions into application-specific exceptions.

### retryWhen

Retries the upstream operation according to a retry policy.

```java
getProduct()
    .retryWhen(
        Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofMillis(200))
    );
```

This retries eligible failures up to three times, using exponential backoff.

Retries can repeat side effects, so they should be used carefully.

### timeout

Applies a time limit to a reactive operation.

```java
getProduct()
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2));
```

If the operation does not produce the required signal within the timeout, Reactor emits a timeout error.

A fallback can be added:

```java
getProduct()
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .onErrorResume(error -> getFallbackProduct());
```

**Conclusion:** Reactive errors are delivered through error signals rather than ordinary synchronous exception propagation. Reactor provides operators such as `onErrorReturn`, `onErrorResume`, `onErrorMap`, `retryWhen`, and `timeout` to handle these errors.

