## 10. Compare Spring MVC and Spring WebFlux.

Spring MVC and Spring WebFlux are two web frameworks provided by Spring.

### Spring MVC

Spring MVC is traditionally based on the Servlet API and an imperative programming model.

A typical controller looks like:

```java
@GetMapping("/students/{id}")
public Student getStudent(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return studentService.findById(id);
}
```

The method returns a regular Java object.

Traditional synchronous request processing commonly uses a thread-per-request model.

Spring MVC works naturally with blocking technologies such as JDBC and JPA.

### Spring WebFlux

Spring WebFlux supports reactive programming and non-blocking I/O.

A controller may return Mono or Flux.

```java
@GetMapping("/students/{id}")
public Mono<Student> getStudent(
        @PathVariable Long id) {

    return studentService.findById(id);
}
```

WebFlux is especially useful for applications with high concurrency and asynchronous I/O operations.

It works naturally with reactive clients and drivers, such as WebClient and R2DBC.

### Comparison

| Feature | Spring MVC | Spring WebFlux |
|---|---|---|
| Programming model | Primarily imperative | Reactive |
| Typical return type | Object, List | Mono, Flux |
| I/O model | Commonly blocking | Supports non-blocking |
| Database integration | JDBC, JPA | R2DBC, reactive drivers |
| Learning curve | Lower | Higher |
| Backpressure | Not central to MVC model | Supported through Reactive Streams |

### When is Spring MVC with virtual threads a better choice?

Java virtual threads allow applications to support many concurrent blocking operations without requiring a dedicated expensive platform thread for each request.

Spring MVC with virtual threads can be a better choice when:

1. The application already uses JDBC or JPA.
2. Most code is written in an imperative style.
3. The team wants to avoid rewriting the application using Mono and Flux.
4. The application performs many blocking I/O operations.
5. Simpler debugging and maintenance are important.

For example:

```java
@GetMapping("/products/{id}")
public Product getProduct(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return productRepository.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow();
}
```

With virtual threads, blocking operations can often scale to higher concurrency while preserving straightforward synchronous code.

However, virtual threads do not remove database connection limits or make blocking JDBC calls non-blocking.

WebFlux may still be preferable when the application requires end-to-end non-blocking I/O, reactive streaming, or explicit backpressure handling.

**Conclusion:** Spring MVC with virtual threads is often a practical choice for existing blocking applications, while WebFlux is useful for systems designed around non-blocking asynchronous processing.

